1
00:00:00,041 --> 00:00:04,747
(Q31) It’s quite hard to think of a city that doesn’t have a big river running through it.

2
00:00:04,747 --> 00:00:12,895
If you think about the major cities in the world, Shanghai, New York, Mumbai, London, they’re nearly all built on rivers.

3
00:00:12,895 --> 00:00:20,770
When these cities were established hundreds or even thousands of years ago, the rivers were a big part of people’s lives.

4
00:00:20,770 --> 00:00:27,338
In 16th-century London, the quickest way to get from one part of the city to another was by river.

5
00:00:27,338 --> 00:00:41,266
But people also used the river for fishing, as the water then was relatively clean, and they would also go on boat trips up and down the river just for pleasure, as a relaxing escape from the noise and bustle of the city streets.

6
00:00:41,266 --> 00:00:47,136
But as industries developed and populations increased, city rivers suffered.

7
00:00:47,136 --> 00:00:53,485
The rising number of people meant there was a huge increase in the amount of sewage discharged into the rivers.

8
00:00:53,485 --> 00:01:01,273
Rivers had always been used for this purpose, but when the number of inhabitants was so small that wasn’t such a problem.

9
00:01:01,273 --> 00:01:08,315
However, as cities grew to over a million inhabitants, the impact on the rivers became more serious.

10
00:01:08,315 --> 00:01:18,024
[Q31答案句，并列符号“-”] In addition, other types of pollution increased as factories were built beside the river and discharged their waste materials into the water.

11
00:01:18,024 --> 00:01:20,443
(Q32) This got worse over time.

12
00:01:20,443 --> 00:01:34,254
[Q32答案句] As recently as 1957, scientists at London’s Natural History Museum declared that the River Thames was ‘dead’ in biological terms, as the water was too filthy to support any kind of life.

13
00:01:34,254 --> 00:01:44,505
(Q33) But in recent years, as rivers lost their industrial function, cities have begun to recognise their true value and to take steps to clean them up.

14
00:01:44,505 --> 00:01:50,213
For example, the River Thames is now cleaner than it’s been for a hundred and fifty years.

15
00:01:50,213 --> 00:02:01,141
[Q33答案句] These days you can see seals swimming in the water and recently people had to try to rescue a whale which had got lost and swum up the river from the sea by mistake.

16
00:02:01,141 --> 00:02:08,666
(Q34) Unfortunately they didn’t succeed, but the problem was disorientation rather than the quality of the water.

17
00:02:08,666 --> 00:02:15,424
Then, all around the world, riverside areas are now seen as prime sites for development.

18
00:02:15,424 --> 00:02:28,942
[Q34答案句： and also = and 并列] Warehouses that were once used for storing goods are now being converted into expensive restaurants and also into apartments with river views, which are in great demand and sell for astronomical prices.

19
00:02:28,942 --> 00:02:43,873
(Q35) [Q35答案句] In Los Angeles, on the west coast of the USA, an architect has plans to revitalise the banks of the river and to make a park there which can provide facilities for sports as well as a natural environment for relaxing in.

20
00:02:43,873 --> 00:02:59,391
(Q36) [Q36答案句] It’s also hoped that the riverside can be used for other purposes – it’s been proposed that facilities could be provided for displaying projects related to various kinds of art that have been produced by local people, for example.

21
00:02:59,391 --> 00:03:19,359
(Q37) [Q37答案句] In the city of Paris, during the summer months of July and August, all the traffic is banned from the roads by the sides of the river and the banks are transformed into beaches, where people can relax in deckchairs under potted palm trees, sunbathe, or buy a drink or a snack while enjoying the view.

22
00:03:19,359 --> 00:03:32,831
(Q38) But to make the most of our rivers in our increasingly crowded cities, we need to allow them to regain their original purpose and be used as a means of transport, reclaiming our streets from cars and lorries.

23
00:03:32,831 --> 00:03:43,586
To do this we’ll have to shift more traffic back to the river, but this time cleanly and silently, making the most of modern technology.

24
00:03:43,586 --> 00:03:53,725
[Q38答案句] Already more than two billion passengers use the ferry to travel in cities around the world like Istanbul, San Francisco and New York.

25
00:03:53,725 --> 00:03:56,392
(Q39) And these numbers are set to rise further.

26
00:03:56,392 --> 00:04:02,672
Admittedly, it’s not a fast way of travelling, but neither is a car when it’s stuck in traffic.

27
00:04:02,672 --> 00:04:09,076
Of course, passenger traffic on roads might decrease as more people start working from home.

28
00:04:09,076 --> 00:04:19,724
But another recent development, the huge rise in online shopping, has meant that another form of urban traffic just keeps on growing, and that’s deliveries.

29
00:04:19,724 --> 00:04:25,003
Trucks and vans in the city pollute and double park while dropping off parcels.

30
00:04:25,003 --> 00:04:31,042
Imagine using the immense capacity of shipping to take these trucks off the road.

31
00:04:31,042 --> 00:04:39,296
One freight barge can replace 44 large trucks, uses far less energy and causes less pollution.

32
00:04:39,296 --> 00:04:49,917
[Q39答案句， 相邻两个词都像答案，选哪个——核心名词cargo] When the barge docks at the riverside, the parcels could be taken the last few kilometres to their final destination on cargo bikes – electric ones, of course.

33
00:04:49,917 --> 00:04:53,103
(Q40) This is already happening in the Dutch city of Amsterdam.

34
00:04:53,103 --> 00:05:00,592
[Q40答案句] And in future, the final stage could even be carried out by drone, although at present this isn’t allowed.

35
00:05:00,592 --> 00:05:04,976
Wouldn’t it be great to unblock our city centres in this way!

36
00:05:04,976 --> 00:05:07,476
Looking further ahead …

